---
/*
  About — bio, the community-organizing to faculty arc, teaching, link to
  the CV page, consulting (RCRC). Per the sitemap (revised 2026-07-27: the
  CV is an HTML page at /cv, not a PDF).

  Every date, title, and organization here comes from Jackson or a verified
  source recorded in AGENTS.md — nothing is inferred. Full-page line edit
  with Jackson 2026-07-28: GCF role now leads and the title question is
  settled ("instructor," not "research faculty"); Bio paragraph 3 rebuilt
  on a means-to-end pattern (bio-research session, modeled on Sarah
  Williams / MIT); MCSC board dates corrected (board July 2023, after the
  ED term — supersedes the July 2021 answer); SVS engagement dateless in
  prose (2022 on the CV); the CAP whitepaper generalized to "published work
  and invited presentations in national forums"; Teaching expanded from the
  Fall 2025 / Spring 2026 syllabi (capstone is 484R per Jackson, though the
  syllabus header says 484); Bozeman line removed from the Bio. RCRC
  "partner" and "consulting arm" phrasing is Jackson's (reconfirmed
  2026-07-28; independence line considered and declined).
*/
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../site';
---

<Base
	title="About"
	description="Jackson Rose is Assistant Director and Operations Manager of the MSU Geospatial Core Facility, an instructor in Earth Sciences at Montana State University, and a consultant on the community dimensions of energy and extractive development."
>
	<h1 class="text-4xl">About</h1>
	<p class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted">{site.roles}</p>

	<section class="mt-10">
		<h2 class="text-3xl">Bio</h2>
		<p class="mt-4">
			Jackson Rose is Assistant Director and Operations Manager of the
			Montana State University Geospatial Core Facility, which provides
			professional GIS services to the campus research community and
			external collaborators, and an instructor in MSU's Department of
			Earth Sciences.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4">
			His research and consulting focus on the economic and social
			dimensions of extractive and energy-sector development: how mines
			and energy projects change the fiscal ground under rural
			communities, and how communities can negotiate durable benefit
			from them. For nearly a decade his work has centered on community
			benefits agreements, from a master's thesis on non-regulatory
			agreements at modern mineral mines, to CBA engagements in Montana
			and Idaho, to published work and invited presentations in national
			forums on mining reform and community benefits.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4">
			He pairs that work with professional GIS practice, using spatial
			and statistical analysis (Python, ArcGIS Pro, open-source GIS),
			geospatial data management, and UAS and LiDAR collection to put
			concrete numbers and maps under the questions rural communities
			face about energy and extractive development. He is a Certified
			GIS Professional and holds an FAA Part 107 remote pilot
			certificate.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12 border-t border-line pt-8">
		<h2 class="text-3xl">Background</h2>
		<p class="mt-4">
			Before joining MSU's faculty, Jackson ran a community organization
			at the center of negotiations over a controversial copper mining
			project in central Montana. From 2021 to 2023 he was Executive
			Director of the Meagher County Stewardship Council, a grassroots
			501(c)(3) in White Sulphur Springs, where he helped lead
			negotiations with an international mining company over a community
			benefits agreement tied to the Black Butte Copper Project. He has
			served on the Council's board since 2023 and has continued helping
			lead the negotiation; in March 2026 the parties announced a
			framework for a binding agreement covering a long-term community
			investment fund, economic development lending, educational
			programming, and environmental stewardship.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4">
			Over the same period he consulted for Salmon Valley Stewardship in
			Salmon, Idaho, advising on the structure and mechanisms of a
			potential community benefits agreement tied to the Idaho Cobalt
			Operations.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4">
			Jackson joined Montana State University in January 2023. He holds
			a B.S. (2017) and an M.S. (2020) in Earth Sciences from MSU, where
			his master's research examined the local costs and benefits of
			modern mineral mines and the role of non-regulatory agreements. He
			has presented this work to the National Academies of Sciences,
			Engineering, and Medicine (2024) and in a U.S. Department of
			Energy / U.S. Energy Association webinar on community benefits
			plans (2023).
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12 border-t border-line pt-8">
		<h2 class="text-3xl">Teaching</h2>
		<p class="mt-4">
			Jackson teaches the advanced and capstone courses in the
			Department of Earth Sciences' three-course GIS sequence, along
			with the graduate companion course.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4">
			<strong>GPHY 384 — Advanced Geographic Information Science and
			Spatial Analysis.</strong> The second course in the sequence:
			spatial data models, analytical methods, error and uncertainty,
			database design, and Python in Jupyter notebooks, with weekly labs
			in both proprietary and open-source software.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4">
			<strong>GPHY 504 — GIS Research Fundamentals.</strong> The
			graduate companion, co-convened with 384 and designed for graduate
			students new to GIS. It adds a weekly recitation where students
			lead discussions of spatial-science literature from their own
			fields, and a project tied to each student's research program.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-4">
			<strong>GPHY 484R — Applied GIS and Spatial Analysis.</strong> The
			capstone. One half is quantitative and statistical analysis of
			geospatial data; the other is a semester-long team project for a
			real client, ending in professional deliverables and each
			student's own portfolio.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12 border-t border-line pt-8">
		<h2 class="text-3xl">CV</h2>
		<p class="mt-4">
			A selected CV — appointments, education, certifications,
			publications, presentations, and service — is on the
			<a href="/cv">CV page</a>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="mt-12 border-t border-line pt-8">
		<h2 class="text-3xl">Consulting</h2>
		<p class="mt-4">
			Jackson is a partner with <a href="https://rcrcgroup.org">Resources
			and Communities Research &amp; Consulting</a> (RCRC), the
			consulting arm of the Resources and Communities Research Group,
			delivering policy-relevant work on community, natural resource,
			and development challenges for governments, community
			organizations, and coalitions across the American West.
		</p>
	</section>
</Base>
